1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
So welcome back.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,040
It's a great honor to introduce a mathematical legend.

3
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:25,080
John Milnor, who as an undergraduate showed that the integral of the curvature of a knot

4
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:34,120
was at least four pi, and became very famous as a result,

5
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:42,120
won the Fields Medal at the age of 31, was professor at Princeton and the Institute for Advanced Study,

6
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:48,120
and is now director of the Center for Mathematical Research at Stony Brook,

7
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:54,120
and is a recipient of many honors, including the National Medal of Science,

8
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:58,160
We'll introduce the next speaker.

9
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:05,160
Jack Milnor, go ahead.

10
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:10,160
Well, it's a great pleasure to introduce Vladimir Voevodsky.

11
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:16,160
Voevodsky began his mathematical career in Moscow, and then came here to Harvard as a junior fellow,

12
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:23,160
and after a few years at Northwestern, is now at the Institute for Advanced Study.

13
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:30,200
And as you all know, he was recently awarded a Fields Medal for his work on motivic homotopy theory.

14
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:39,200
This is a marvelous development which introduces ideas and methods from homotopy theory into pure algebra,

15
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:45,200
and gives an essential new tool for solving algebraic problems.

16
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,200
So it's a great pleasure to introduce Voevodsky.

17
00:01:49,240 --> 00:02:02,240
Hello, thank you very much for, thank you, Jack, for the introduction.

18
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:09,240
So I was given by Arthur sort of an impossible task.

19
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:17,240
I was requested to explain motivic homotopy theory so that it will be understood by non-mathematicians.

20
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:21,280
So whatever came out of it, you'll see now,

21
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:31,280
but I don't guarantee that it will be now understandable by either mathematicians or non-mathematicians.

22
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:42,280
So what there was at first, so at first there were spaces.

23
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:49,320
So mathematicians were studying spaces and starting with simple examples of spaces.

24
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:52,320
So one of the most basic spaces is a point.

25
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:56,320
There is one which is even more basic, but it's hard to draw.

26
00:02:56,320 --> 00:03:03,320
Another one is standard space, which I will have to talk about, is a unit interval.

27
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:07,320
So an interval of the real line, a line between 0 and 1.

28
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:15,360
The third example here on the picture is some example of a space of dimension 2.

29
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:26,360
It's supposed to look like some baking good with two holes in it.

30
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:32,360
So the last example is more sophisticated, and I want to include it here.

31
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,360
It's a more sort of mathematical example of a space.

32
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:42,400
It's a space of all n by n matrices with complex entries and with non-zero determinant.

33
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,400
That would be a sophisticated space of dimension n, well,

34
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:52,400
depending on how you count, n square or 2n square.

35
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:58,400
So there are many different axiomatizations of the notion of a space in mathematics.

36
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,400
For instance, there are topological spaces, there are metric spaces.

37
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:04,440
At the end, it all doesn't matter.

38
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:11,440
As long as one only considers non-pathological examples,

39
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:13,440
it all comes to the same thing.

40
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:20,440
So I will assume that there is some intuitive understanding of what space is.

41
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:25,440
That such understanding is present.

42
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:33,480
So any space, if you formalize it, really write down a definition,

43
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:35,480
it will be a tremendously infinite object.

44
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:37,480
So it's very difficult to work with.

45
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:44,480
So to distinguish spaces mathematically, we use what we call invariance.

46
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:49,480
So something more understandable, more finite, typically,

47
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:52,480
than the space itself associated to a space.

48
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:58,520
So there are two invariants which I will dare to call the most fundamental ones.

49
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,520
One is the dimension of a space.

50
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:08,520
So we have dimension zero for a point and dimension one for the interval.

51
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:18,520
And another invariant is the number of pieces the space consists of.

52
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:24,560
So in this picture, again, the point consists of one piece.

53
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:27,560
You cannot divide it into two disconnected pieces.

54
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:33,560
And then there is something below the point which is sort of a deformed circle

55
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:36,560
with some deformed interval sitting inside,

56
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,560
but it consists of two different pieces, two disjoint pieces.

57
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:41,560
There's a circle and there's an interval.

58
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:44,560
So the number of pieces is another invariant of a space.

59
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:46,560
Typically, when one thinks of a space,

60
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:49,600
one thinks of a space which has only one piece.

61
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:56,600
But that's just a custom.

62
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:02,600
So what is homotopy theory about?

63
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:10,600
What it does, it basically studies...

64
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,600
Oh, I need to say something.

65
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:20,640
Let's call pi zero over space the set of its pieces.

66
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:24,640
So if space has two pieces, then pi zero is a set of two elements,

67
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,640
namely these pieces.

68
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:29,640
So it's typically much more finite than the space itself.

69
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:34,640
What homotopy theory does, it studies some fancy versions of this invariant,

70
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,640
which are called higher homotopy sets.

71
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:42,680
Now, I will try to explain how one gets to these pi n's,

72
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:47,680
where pi n will be some set for any integer n,

73
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,680
for any, excuse me, non-negative integer n,

74
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:53,680
how one gets to pi n from pi zero.

75
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:56,680
The first thing which I need to...

76
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:58,680
It can be done in different ways.

77
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:03,680
So I choose to base my explanation on the notion of a continuous map,

78
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:07,720
which will also play a fundamental role further.

79
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:09,720
So what is a continuous map?

80
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,720
So I have a space.

81
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:13,720
This I cannot show on the projection.

82
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:17,720
I have a space n, m, and a space n.

83
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:20,720
And a map from one to another is any rule

84
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:22,720
which assigns to point of the first space

85
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:24,720
and point of the second space.

86
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:26,720
The easiest way, of course, to understand it

87
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:29,720
is by looking at examples.

88
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:31,760
And the idea that it's continuous means

89
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,760
that if something was close,

90
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,760
then it doesn't get too far apart.

91
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,760
So let's denote by c of m, n

92
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:48,760
the set of all continuous maps from m to n.

93
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,760
And we'll see again, by example, what it is.

94
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:52,760
If I'm looking at continuous maps

95
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,760
from a point to any space,

96
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:58,760
so what is a map from a point to a space?

97
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:02,800
It's just determined by where this unique point goes.

98
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:04,800
So a map from a point to a space

99
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,800
is the same as a point on this space.

100
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,800
So the set of maps from the point to any space

101
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:13,800
is just a set of points of this very same space.

102
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:16,800
Another example is what is the set of maps

103
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,800
from any space to a point?

104
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,800
There's only one place for all the points

105
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:23,800
of the first space to go,

106
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:27,800
namely this only point on the target space.

107
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:30,840
And there's always only one map

108
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:32,840
from any space to the point.

109
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:35,840
So that's example number two.

110
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:39,840
Here is a more fancy example.

111
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:42,840
Let's look at maps

112
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:46,840
from the unit interval,

113
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:48,840
the interval of the real line,

114
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:50,840
to any space.

115
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:54,840
So it will have some beginning

116
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:56,840
where the point zero goes

117
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:57,880
or point one goes,

118
00:08:57,880 --> 00:08:59,880
and then there will be all the points

119
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:02,880
where all the numbers between zero and one go.

120
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:04,880
So the fact that it's a continuous map

121
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:08,880
means that this gadget will have no breaks in it.

122
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,880
That's what it intuitively means.

123
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,880
So the set of all continuous maps

124
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:15,880
from the interval to any space

125
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:19,880
is just a set of paths inside this space.

126
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:29,840
Now let's go back to the notion

127
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:32,840
of the number of pieces of a space.

128
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,840
How can we say formally

129
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:40,840
when two points lie in the same piece?

130
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:42,840
So if they lie in the same piece,

131
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:44,840
then they can be connected by a path.

132
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:46,840
In different pieces,

133
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:48,840
there's no way you can find a path

134
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:51,840
without breaks which connects one point to another.

135
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:54,840
So to define formally

136
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:57,840
what it means for two points to belong to the same piece,

137
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:59,840
one can use this notion of a path,

138
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:02,840
of a continuous map from interval to the space.

139
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:09,840
So let me repeat myself again.

140
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:12,840
So the only thing which one needs to know

141
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:17,840
to define the set pi zero of n,

142
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:19,840
the set of pieces,

143
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:22,840
is to know what are points and what are paths

144
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:25,840
between these points.

145
00:10:30,840 --> 00:10:33,840
Well, this is a little bit more fancy,

146
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:37,840
and maybe I'm not able to explain it as good.

147
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:40,840
Now here I'm moving to the next invariant,

148
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:42,840
next homotopy set pi one.

149
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:44,840
As I said, pi zero is the starting point

150
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:48,840
of an infinite series of homotopy sets.

151
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:51,840
So what one does for pi zero,

152
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:53,840
one takes points

153
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:57,840
and identifies sort of those

154
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:00,840
which can be connected by a path.

155
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:03,840
Now suppose I fix two points, P and Q.

156
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:06,840
Now let's consider all the paths which connect them.

157
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:08,840
Suppose they land the same piece,

158
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:10,840
so there is some path which connects them.

159
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:13,840
Let's look at all paths.

160
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,840
So we get some space of paths, whatever that means.

161
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:18,840
Let's try to understand how many pieces

162
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:20,840
this space of paths has,

163
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:22,840
and call this set of pieces the pi one

164
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:26,840
of my original space relative to points P and Q.

165
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:30,840
So in practice what one has to do

166
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:33,840
is to consider now continuous maps from a square.

167
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:38,840
If I have a square mapping to the space,

168
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:43,840
and let's say I request to have a map

169
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:47,840
which contracts this side completely to point P

170
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:50,840
and this side completely to point Q.

171
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:53,840
Then the image of this side will be a path

172
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:55,840
going from P to Q,

173
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,840
and the image of this side will also be a path

174
00:11:57,840 --> 00:12:00,840
which is going from P to Q.

175
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:04,840
And as I move from this side to this side continuously,

176
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:08,840
I can think of it as being a path between the paths.

177
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:11,840
So it's continuous deformation between the paths.

178
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:14,840
So it generates...

179
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:19,840
Now I can consider the set of pieces

180
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:25,840
of the space of paths

181
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:30,840
where two paths are declared

182
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:32,840
to belong to the same piece

183
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:36,840
if they can be connected by such a map from a square.

184
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:39,840
Now this is a procedure which can be repeated,

185
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:44,840
and then I can use cubes to connect maps from squares

186
00:12:44,840 --> 00:12:49,840
and so on, and one gets a sequence of sets

187
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:52,840
which are called homotope sets over space.

188
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:54,840
Now...

189
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:06,840
Well, I'm sorry, that's what I just said.

190
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:14,840
There is one nice example which I want to give.

191
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:20,840
If we try to define these homotope sets

192
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:23,840
for the space of complex matrices

193
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:27,840
with a non-zero determinant,

194
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:29,840
it's, of course, a very complicated problem

195
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:31,840
how to compare the homotope sets

196
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:34,840
with the space of complex matrices

197
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:37,840
with a non-zero determinant.

198
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:39,840
It's, of course, a very complicated problem

199
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:42,840
how to compute such a weird thing for such a weird space.

200
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:44,840
However, it turns out that it can be done,

201
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:47,840
and the answer,

202
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:51,840
at least when we consider matrices which are very, very big,

203
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:54,840
n by n, where n is very large,

204
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:58,840
the answer turns out to be wonderfully simple.

205
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:02,840
It's the first homotope set is the point.

206
00:14:02,840 --> 00:14:04,840
Any two matrices can be connected by a path,

207
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:07,840
and there's only one piece in the whole space of matrices.

208
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:12,840
If I consider paths,

209
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:17,840
then it turns out that the pieces in the space of paths

210
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:20,840
are numbered by integers.

211
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:23,840
So pi 1 is a set of integers...

212
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:26,840
I'm sorry, did I...

213
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:30,840
I made a mistake writing this page.

214
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:32,840
It has to read like this.

215
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:35,840
Pi 0 is a star.

216
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:37,840
Or did I?

217
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:40,840
No, no, no, sorry.

218
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:44,840
So pi 1 can be identified with the set of integral numbers.

219
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:47,840
Pi 2 is again just one point.

220
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,840
Pi 3 is again the set of integral numbers.

221
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:53,840
And then it goes on like that for a very long time.

222
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:56,840
For how long exactly depends on number n over there.

223
00:14:56,840 --> 00:14:59,840
But basically if n is infinity, then it goes on forever.

224
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:01,840
And that's called what periodicity theorem.

225
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:04,840
There was one of the very impressive achievements

226
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:12,840
of mathematics in early 60s, late 50s.

227
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:15,840
I don't know the history well enough.

228
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,840
But that was one of the really first big computations

229
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:20,840
of homotopy theory.

230
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:29,840
So that's what I wanted to say about homotopy theory.

231
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:31,840
It studies spaces.

232
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:33,840
It associates to them the homotopy sets,

233
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:37,840
which come from studying paths of paths and so on.

234
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:39,840
And here is an example of computation.

235
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:41,840
These are actually things which can be computed

236
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,840
in some non-trivial cases.

237
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:49,840
Now I want to jump somehow and do something similar

238
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:51,840
in algebraic setting.

239
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:54,840
So there is only one way to do it.

240
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:59,840
And I don't know how much I will have time to say today.

241
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:09,840
But there is one topic which I want to talk about very quietly

242
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:11,840
and without hurry.

243
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:15,840
So that's something which I never saw it even mentioned

244
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:18,840
in any popular book about mathematics.

245
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:21,840
I saw algebraic equations mentioned.

246
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:24,840
I saw homotopy groups probably even mentioned,

247
00:16:24,840 --> 00:16:28,840
especially for elementary books in topology.

248
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:33,840
But I think that at the heart of 20th century mathematics

249
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:36,840
lies one particular notion.

250
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:38,840
And that's the notion of a category.

251
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:43,840
So let me try to explain what it is about.

252
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:47,840
So it's a very formal gadget.

253
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:53,840
And I will illustrate it by an example.

254
00:16:57,840 --> 00:16:59,840
So here are two examples of categories.

255
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:02,840
Let's forget about the second one for a moment.

256
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:05,840
Just look at the first one.

257
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:09,840
So here I have a picture of a category with three objects,

258
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:11,840
A, B, C.

259
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:13,840
Now there are arrows between those objects,

260
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:15,840
which are called morphisms.

261
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:18,840
And then there is the rule, which says that if I have one arrow

262
00:17:18,840 --> 00:17:21,840
and then it's followed by another arrow,

263
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:23,840
then there is a third arrow.

264
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,840
Let's say if I have an arrow from A to B

265
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:27,840
and arrow from B to C,

266
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:30,840
then there is a rule to get from such a pair

267
00:17:30,840 --> 00:17:32,840
an arrow from A to C.

268
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:34,840
That's called the composition rule.

269
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:36,840
But let's even forget this third part for a second.

270
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:40,840
It's not necessarily important for intuitive understanding.

271
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:42,840
Main thing which exists in categories

272
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:45,840
is that there are objects and there are morphisms.

273
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:47,840
There is a formal set.

274
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:49,840
In this case, the set of A, B, C,

275
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:51,840
which is called the set of objects.

276
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:53,840
For every pair of objects,

277
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:55,840
there is another formal set.

278
00:17:55,840 --> 00:17:59,840
In this case, it's X and Y between A and B,

279
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:03,840
Z between B and C, and T between A and C,

280
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:06,840
which are called morphisms between these objects.

281
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:09,840
Now the first example is an example

282
00:18:09,840 --> 00:18:12,840
of a combinatorial finite category.

283
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,840
Here is a second example.

284
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:18,840
Objects are spaces, all spaces which exist.

285
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:21,840
Morphisms between two spaces

286
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:24,840
are continuous maps between these spaces.

287
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:27,840
And of course continuous maps can be composed.

288
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,840
So that gives an example of a category

289
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:31,840
of a very different kind.

290
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:37,840
And what was somewhat amazing for me personally

291
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:42,840
and an amazing discovery is that almost,

292
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:46,840
let's say for very many classes of mathematical objects,

293
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:49,840
there are natural categories which correspond to them.

294
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:52,840
So for most classes of mathematical objects,

295
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:57,840
we can construct a category whose objects

296
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:00,840
will be these objects.

297
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:04,840
Morphisms will be some natural ways

298
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:08,840
of connecting one object of this type to another,

299
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:11,840
and there will be some kind of composition.

300
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:21,840
Sorry.

301
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:41,840
So here is a more formal way to see what a category is.

302
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:44,840
So there is a set of objects.

303
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:47,840
For any pair of objects, there is a set of morphisms

304
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:50,840
from this object to, between these two objects.

305
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:53,840
And then there is some composition rule.

306
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:09,840
Now, what I hope to do now is to show how such a,

307
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:12,840
well, let's see, how does it work?

308
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:14,840
Why is it interesting?

309
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:16,840
Let's, for instance, consider the category

310
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:19,840
of topological spaces.

311
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:22,840
And let's take, let's choose a space N.

312
00:20:22,840 --> 00:20:25,840
What can we say about this space?

313
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:29,840
Looking at it exclusively as being an object

314
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:31,840
of the category of spaces.

315
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:33,840
So I don't want to look inside of this space.

316
00:20:33,840 --> 00:20:35,840
I know nothing about its insides.

317
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:40,840
I only know continuous maps relating this space

318
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:42,840
to all other spaces.

319
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:46,840
So instead of looking on the structure of the object,

320
00:20:46,840 --> 00:20:49,840
you look at his sort of social relationship.

321
00:20:52,840 --> 00:20:55,840
The very first example, can we figure out

322
00:20:55,840 --> 00:20:58,840
when the space is a point?

323
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:00,840
To this, there is a nice answer.

324
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:06,840
When a space is a point, if and only if

325
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:10,840
for any other space, there is a single morphism

326
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:12,840
to this one.

327
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:18,840
So that characterizes the point completely,

328
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:24,840
exclusively by morphisms to and from it.

329
00:21:24,840 --> 00:21:27,840
Let's ask a more complicated question.

330
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:29,840
When is a space N connected?

331
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:32,840
So when it consists of only one piece.

332
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:45,840
Or more generally, can we somehow express,

333
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:48,840
let's say the very first homotopia set,

334
00:21:48,840 --> 00:21:52,840
the set of pieces, purely in categorical terms?

335
00:21:52,840 --> 00:21:54,840
It turns out that yes, we can.

336
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:56,840
So let's try to play this game.

337
00:21:56,840 --> 00:21:58,840
So first of all, we know what is the set of points

338
00:21:58,840 --> 00:21:59,840
of the space.

339
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:03,840
It's the set of morphisms from the point to this space.

340
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:06,840
And we have already characterized the point

341
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:09,840
in some internal way.

342
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:13,840
Now, let's consider the unit interval.

343
00:22:13,840 --> 00:22:16,840
The set of maps from the unit interval, morphisms

344
00:22:16,840 --> 00:22:20,840
from the unit interval to the space, is the set of paths.

345
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:26,840
Now, I need to know what is the beginning of the path

346
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:29,840
and what is the end of the path.

347
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:32,840
For that, I need the notion of a composition of morphisms.

348
00:22:32,840 --> 00:22:37,840
So if I have a path, I have two distinguished points, 0 and 1,

349
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:40,840
on the unit interval, which are morphisms from the point

350
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:42,840
to the unit interval.

351
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:46,840
If I look at the composition, my path composed

352
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:50,840
with one of these points, I get a point of the space,

353
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:53,840
which is the beginning or the end of the path.

354
00:22:53,840 --> 00:22:58,840
So using purely the structure of the category,

355
00:22:58,840 --> 00:22:59,840
I know what is the set of points.

356
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:01,840
I know what is the set of paths.

357
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:05,840
And I know how to assign beginning and end to a path.

358
00:23:05,840 --> 00:23:09,840
So now I can say what is the set of pieces,

359
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:14,840
because it's simply I have to identify two points which

360
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:16,840
can be connected by a path, in other words,

361
00:23:16,840 --> 00:23:19,840
for which there is a path such that it's beginning and end.

362
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:22,840
And that gives me the set pi 0.

363
00:23:22,840 --> 00:23:26,840
So the only thing which is here, which is not internal

364
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:30,840
to the category of categorical structure,

365
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:31,840
is the unit interval itself.

366
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:36,840
We don't know how to characterize it from the inside.

367
00:23:36,840 --> 00:23:40,840
But as soon as I have a category and something which

368
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:44,840
I want to call the unit interval with two maps,

369
00:23:44,840 --> 00:23:46,840
A and B, called beginning and end,

370
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:49,840
I can define some kind of pi 0.

371
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:51,840
How good this definition will be?

372
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:52,840
It's a big question.

373
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:06,840
So similarly, if I want to compute the next homotopy set,

374
00:24:06,840 --> 00:24:09,840
here I'm not going in that much detail.

375
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:17,840
But what I will need to know will be the object point,

376
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:19,840
the object called unit interval.

377
00:24:19,840 --> 00:24:23,840
And then I'll need to know to have some distinguished object

378
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:27,840
which is called the square.

379
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:30,840
And some finite number of morphisms

380
00:24:30,840 --> 00:24:32,840
connecting these objects.

381
00:24:32,840 --> 00:24:35,840
As soon as I have such a purely combinatorial structure

382
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:39,840
inside my category, I can consider morphisms

383
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:43,840
with values in any object and get something which

384
00:24:43,840 --> 00:24:45,840
I will call pi 0 and pi 1.

385
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:49,840
I can continue that and model formally in any category.

386
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:55,840
Basically, for what I'm talking about,

387
00:24:55,840 --> 00:24:57,840
it's enough to understand pi 0.

388
00:24:57,840 --> 00:25:06,840
And the rest is just not to look too elementary.

389
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:09,840
So one can formalize the situation

390
00:25:09,840 --> 00:25:16,840
when one has point, interval, square, cube,

391
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:18,840
cube of the next dimension, and so on.

392
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:22,840
Now all these things in usual topological world

393
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:23,840
map to each other.

394
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:25,840
There are two maps from the point to the interval,

395
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:27,840
the beginning and the end.

396
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:30,840
There are four maps from the interval to the square,

397
00:25:30,840 --> 00:25:32,840
the four sides of the square.

398
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:36,840
There are six maps from the square to the cube,

399
00:25:36,840 --> 00:25:39,840
the six sides of the cube, and so on.

400
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:40,840
They satisfy some relations.

401
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:44,840
So I can formally say, let's take any category whatsoever.

402
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:49,840
Let's take a series of objects and morphisms between them,

403
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,840
satisfying appropriate conditions.

404
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:57,840
Let's call such a gadget a cubical object.

405
00:25:57,840 --> 00:26:00,840
So as soon as I have a cubical object in any category,

406
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:04,840
I can play the game which I sort of explained

407
00:26:04,840 --> 00:26:08,840
for pi 0 and pi 1 and define some kind of homotopy sets

408
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:15,840
for any other object in this category.

409
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:21,840
Of course, it needs to be said that in most cases

410
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:22,840
it's not going to work very well,

411
00:26:22,840 --> 00:26:27,840
but it will work somehow.

412
00:26:27,840 --> 00:26:36,840
Now let's go to the next...

413
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:51,840
So of course that was just an example.

414
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:55,840
In principle, there are many more structures,

415
00:26:55,840 --> 00:26:57,840
let's say in topological spaces,

416
00:26:57,840 --> 00:27:00,840
which are needed for homotopy theory.

417
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:04,840
So one can translate most of the structures

418
00:27:04,840 --> 00:27:06,840
into categorical terms,

419
00:27:06,840 --> 00:27:11,840
as I tried to show in this example of cubical spaces.

420
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:14,840
And then as soon as they are translated

421
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:16,840
into these formal categorical terms,

422
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:19,840
one can try to take some very different category

423
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:22,840
and apply it there and see what happens.

424
00:27:22,840 --> 00:27:24,840
So material homotopy theory happens

425
00:27:24,840 --> 00:27:28,840
when one applies this kind of procedure

426
00:27:28,840 --> 00:27:31,840
to the following category of algebraic equations,

427
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:34,840
systems of algebraic equations,

428
00:27:34,840 --> 00:27:36,840
which I'm going to describe in a moment.

429
00:27:36,840 --> 00:27:40,840
That's not exactly the standard approach to algebraic geometry,

430
00:27:40,840 --> 00:27:45,840
but that's the most elementary one which I could think of.

431
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:48,840
So let's consider the following objects,

432
00:27:48,840 --> 00:27:51,840
which we call systems of algebraic equations.

433
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:53,840
So by definition, a system of algebraic equations

434
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:55,840
is the following gadget.

435
00:27:55,840 --> 00:27:57,840
It's first of all a list of letters

436
00:27:57,840 --> 00:27:59,840
which are called variables of the system,

437
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:03,840
and second of all some list of polynomials in these variables

438
00:28:03,840 --> 00:28:06,840
which are called equations of the system.

439
00:28:06,840 --> 00:28:08,840
So here is an example.

440
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:10,840
There is one variable X

441
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:13,840
and one equation X squared plus one.

442
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:15,840
Here is another example.

443
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:18,840
There are N variables, X1, XN,

444
00:28:18,840 --> 00:28:21,840
and there are no equations whatsoever.

445
00:28:21,840 --> 00:28:25,840
That's a very important system which is denoted by AN.

446
00:28:27,840 --> 00:28:30,840
Here is a more sophisticated example.

447
00:28:30,840 --> 00:28:34,840
I'm sorry, I was getting late writing it,

448
00:28:34,840 --> 00:28:37,840
and it's not probably very readable.

449
00:28:37,840 --> 00:28:41,840
I have N squared variables.

450
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:46,840
I'm thinking about them as about entries in a matrix

451
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:49,840
plus one additional variable which I call T.

452
00:28:49,840 --> 00:28:51,840
And I have one equation which says

453
00:28:51,840 --> 00:28:55,840
it's the determinant of this matrix times T minus one.

454
00:28:55,840 --> 00:28:58,840
Well, it doesn't say anything, but that's my equation.

455
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:01,840
That's my polynomial.

456
00:29:01,840 --> 00:29:05,840
And such a system of equations I want to denote by GLN.

457
00:29:06,840 --> 00:29:10,840
Now, let's define morphisms.

458
00:29:15,840 --> 00:29:18,840
Oh, I need to say something else.

459
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:22,840
So now I want to try to define

460
00:29:22,840 --> 00:29:26,840
the category of systems of algebraic equations.

461
00:29:28,840 --> 00:29:31,840
So what I need first are

462
00:29:31,840 --> 00:29:34,840
objects, and as objects I will take...

463
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:36,840
I want to sort of say let's consider

464
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:38,840
all systems of algebraic equations,

465
00:29:38,840 --> 00:29:40,840
but that's a bit too many.

466
00:29:40,840 --> 00:29:44,840
So a typical approach is we first choose

467
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:47,840
what kind of coefficients are we going to consider.

468
00:29:47,840 --> 00:29:51,840
So let's take some system of coefficients,

469
00:29:51,840 --> 00:29:55,840
integral numbers or real numbers or complex numbers,

470
00:29:55,840 --> 00:29:59,840
and consider all systems with the same coefficients.

471
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:01,840
And consider all systems with coefficients

472
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:05,840
in this type of numbers.

473
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:09,840
That will be the class of objects of our category.

474
00:30:11,840 --> 00:30:14,840
Now I need morphisms from one system to another.

475
00:30:14,840 --> 00:30:17,840
So I tried to write it down,

476
00:30:17,840 --> 00:30:22,840
and it's difficult to read on a projector.

477
00:30:22,840 --> 00:30:27,840
So by morphism I want to mean a change of variables.

478
00:30:27,840 --> 00:30:31,840
So a morphism from one system in variables x

479
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:34,840
to another system in variables y

480
00:30:34,840 --> 00:30:37,840
is going to be some way to express variables y

481
00:30:37,840 --> 00:30:41,840
through variables x, such that the equations

482
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:45,840
sort of are satisfied on both sides.

483
00:30:45,840 --> 00:30:48,840
There is some obvious way to compose these things.

484
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:50,840
It wasn't I who invented this.

485
00:30:50,840 --> 00:30:52,840
I don't know who invented it,

486
00:30:52,840 --> 00:30:54,840
but I read it in a wonderful textbook

487
00:30:54,840 --> 00:30:58,840
by Manin on algebraic geometry,

488
00:30:58,840 --> 00:31:00,840
which was published in Soviet Union

489
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:03,840
in the end of the 60s.

490
00:31:05,840 --> 00:31:09,840
So here is an example.

491
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:11,840
Let's consider A0.

492
00:31:11,840 --> 00:31:14,840
So remember A0 is...

493
00:31:16,840 --> 00:31:18,840
Let me do it this way.

494
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:27,840
A0 is a system of no equations and no variables.

495
00:31:27,840 --> 00:31:32,840
So a morphism from such a system

496
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:40,840
would mean that I have to find expressions of my x1, xn

497
00:31:40,840 --> 00:31:42,840
in terms of no variables whatsoever.

498
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:45,840
So it means I have to replace them by constants,

499
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:47,840
and the equations still have to be satisfied.

500
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:50,840
So such a morphism is exactly a solution

501
00:31:50,840 --> 00:31:52,840
of my system of equations.

502
00:31:52,840 --> 00:31:54,840
So morphisms from A0 to x

503
00:31:54,840 --> 00:31:57,840
are solutions of the system of equations.

504
00:31:57,840 --> 00:32:02,840
Similarly, one can verify that morphisms from x to A0,

505
00:32:02,840 --> 00:32:05,840
there's always only one morphism.

506
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:09,840
So these objects An,

507
00:32:09,840 --> 00:32:15,840
these systems sort of with no equations,

508
00:32:15,840 --> 00:32:18,840
they form a cubical object in a very naive sense.

509
00:32:18,840 --> 00:32:20,840
One can easily build all the maps

510
00:32:20,840 --> 00:32:23,840
which exist between usual cubes.

511
00:32:23,840 --> 00:32:26,840
One can build them between these systems.

512
00:32:26,840 --> 00:32:28,840
And so I can use this cubical object

513
00:32:28,840 --> 00:32:30,840
to define some kind of homotypic groups

514
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:34,840
for any system of algebraic equations.

515
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:37,840
In general, I will get...

516
00:32:37,840 --> 00:32:39,840
Sorry, homotypic sets.

517
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:44,840
In general, I will get not quite correct results,

518
00:32:44,840 --> 00:32:47,840
but there is an example.

519
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:51,840
If I apply it to the system GLN,

520
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:54,840
I will actually get very, very reasonable things.

521
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:59,840
I will get sets which are called algebraic K groups

522
00:32:59,840 --> 00:33:02,840
of the original system of coefficients.

523
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:08,840
And these groups were defined in many difficult ways

524
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:15,840
over the last, I don't know, 40 years.

525
00:33:15,840 --> 00:33:18,840
And they're still a source of great many

526
00:33:18,840 --> 00:33:22,840
interesting mathematical problems and insights.

527
00:33:22,840 --> 00:33:25,840
This is something which really depends on key.

528
00:33:25,840 --> 00:33:29,840
So for integers, it will have something to do

529
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:32,840
with number theory and arithmetics.

530
00:33:32,840 --> 00:33:36,840
For complex numbers or real numbers,

531
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:41,840
there are connections with special functions

532
00:33:41,840 --> 00:33:44,840
such as polylogarism and hyperbolic geometry,

533
00:33:44,840 --> 00:33:46,840
and I don't know what else.

534
00:33:46,840 --> 00:33:48,840
So these are very, very interesting groups.

535
00:33:48,840 --> 00:33:56,840
And sort of this is an example of how such a game

536
00:33:56,840 --> 00:33:59,840
can lead us to actually objects

537
00:33:59,840 --> 00:34:01,840
which are of independent interest.

538
00:34:02,840 --> 00:34:11,840
So now let me have the last slide

539
00:34:11,840 --> 00:34:14,840
and get a little philosophical.

540
00:34:14,840 --> 00:34:19,840
And so that's how I see the whole picture.

541
00:34:19,840 --> 00:34:22,840
Let me start with geometry,

542
00:34:22,840 --> 00:34:25,840
category of topological spaces.

543
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:28,840
We invent something about this geometrical world

544
00:34:28,840 --> 00:34:31,840
using our basically visual intuition.

545
00:34:31,840 --> 00:34:34,840
The notion of pieces comes exclusively

546
00:34:34,840 --> 00:34:37,840
from visual intuition.

547
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:40,840
We're somehow abstracted and rewrite it

548
00:34:40,840 --> 00:34:42,840
in terms of category theory,

549
00:34:42,840 --> 00:34:45,840
which provides this connecting language.

550
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:48,840
And then we apply it in a new situation,

551
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:50,840
in this case in the situation of algebraic equations,

552
00:34:50,840 --> 00:34:52,840
which is purely algebraic.

553
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:54,840
So what we get is some fantastic way

554
00:34:54,840 --> 00:34:56,840
to translate geometric intuition

555
00:34:56,840 --> 00:35:01,840
into results about algebraic objects.

556
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:03,840
And that's, from my point of view,

557
00:35:03,840 --> 00:35:06,840
the main fun of doing mathematics.

558
00:35:06,840 --> 00:35:08,840
Thank you.

559
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:17,840
Applause

